# How to adjust the quest design?
The games quest flow feels like a linear to-do list. In order to change that, increase player's agency.

1. Give feedback when player prefinished future quests.
2. For their firsts give immediate feedback.
3. Articulate the quests as intentions rather then literal work tasks.
4. Add hint systems. Instead text use camera zooms to objectives and short anim clips.

# How to determine Quest text tone?
Rather than making it feel funtional use a little humor and playful punchy text to follow the mood of game. 

# What did I learn designing the game?
- When coming up with ideas use as a base another positive exmaple from something familiar and think how to bring in a little twist to it. 90% base + 10% own idea.
Most of the times creating something completely new takes a lot of research and iteration.
- Rather work with visual feedback rather then text (most people don't like to read)
- keep player agency high

+ What worked well
- the in-game editor for env building
- generating imgs and translating it to ascii/glyph design

# What to expect from Usertesting?

I would structure your actual empirical analysis like this:
Primary
A vs. B perceived control
Paired Likert comparison + direct comparative question.
Secondary
A vs. B expectation management
A vs. B handling of unexpected output/frustration
A vs. B willingness to reuse / overall preference
Timing
Preference for before / after / both / neither
- usefulness ratings for pre-clarification and post-feedback.
Overall UX
Context
AI usage
Gaming frequency
Cozy-game interest
Age
Profession
Qualitative explanation
One open preference reason → thematic categories.
Experimental check
AB vs BA → inspect for obvious order effects.


